  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Discreet and barely visible from the road, Pramousquier beach spreads out as an expanse of fine white sand and peacock-blue water, in the sheltered bay between Cap Nègre (which protects it from the Mistral) and Pointe de l'Écuelle. On the night of 14 to 15 August 1944, the Commandos d'Afrique climbed the rocks of Cap Nègre at Pramousquier, where a plaque recalls their landing (Le Lavandou tourist office).</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The beach is shared between two towns, which do not have the same rule. On the Rayol-Canadel-sur-Mer side, dogs on a leash are allowed from 1 June to 30 September between 7pm and 8am only (town hall, 23 June 2026). On the Le Lavandou side, which ranks Pramousquier among its "12 beaches", the town hall's official page says beach access is "strictly forbidden to dogs and all animals from 1 April to 30 October", while the Le Lavandou tourist office page says dogs are allowed from November to March. To settle in with your dog in summer, therefore stay on the Rayol-Canadel side and respect the 7pm-8am hours; check the signs on site to spot the boundary between the two towns.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the Voie Verte (former railway line) from Canadel is a car-free route on foot or by bike (about 2 km according to the tourist office); the town reminds visitors that pedestrians have priority on the Voie Verte, so keep your dog close.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p><a href="/en/fiche-canadel">Canadel beach</a> is 2 km away along the Voie Verte.</p>
    </div>

